Stepping back from the individual fixes, I think it is worth naming what Trustless AI gets if this v1 lands in the shape we have converged on.
This stops being just a PQ key-rotation repair.
It becomes a reusable admit → enumerate → commit → anchor → verify primitive for state transitions.
The important shift is that every verdict-bearing dependency moves out of implementation state:
ordering is derived from the committed admission sequence, not trusted key_epoch;
authorization is proved separately by the preserved owner-signed transition;
completeness is admission-relative and mechanically checkable against an anchored admission head;
batch coverage is committed through an indexed sequence and manifest, not inferred from a bare Merkle root;
construction version is itself commitment-bearing;
governs_from is derived from externally witnessed anchors, not stored timestamps;
equivocation, conflict and liveness failure become distinguishable, independently provable facts.
The signed acknowledgement also changes the accountability model. Once the producer signs the exact acceptance_cc, an owner can hold evidence of an admission before external closure, and two incompatible acknowledgements are already enough to prove ACK_EQUIVOCATION without waiting for a chain event.
The boundary stays exact: this proves completeness of the admitted set, not completeness of every request or intent. Refusal before admission remains a censorship/admission-policy question, not something the sequence can magically prove away.
What I like most is that this no longer looks bespoke to PQ bindings. The same structural primitive should apply to other lifecycle surfaces where Trustless AI needs third parties to distinguish:
not yet visible from provably omitted,
timing failure from semantic invalidity,
claimed order from committed order,
and membership from completeness.
So the deliverable is larger than “v1 key binding”: it is a reference architecture for recomputable, auditable state-transition infrastructure.
I would freeze the design before extending it further now: finish the rewritten spec, attack it with adversarial vectors, then implement against that frozen shape.
